{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Michel Della Negra",
          "Peter Jenni",
          "Tejinder Virdee"
        ],
        "citation": "For the conception and construction of the ATLAS and CMS experiments, which enabled the discovery of the Higgs boson."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B008",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The Higgs discovery confirmed a central prediction of the Standard Model and has been independently established and studied over many years. The case for these three leaders is strongest when the achievement is framed around conceiving and building two experiments capable of making the discovery, rather than assigning them sole credit for the collaborations’ result. That experimental foundation is a major and mature contribution in its own right.",
    "case_against": "ATLAS and CMS depended on very large collaborations, and the supplied assessments do not resolve how to distinguish the named leaders’ individual contributions from the indispensable work of those teams. A citation that implies they alone made the discovery would overstate the attribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "He notes that the Higgs signal has been independently established and extensively characterized, while the relative individual contributions to the experiments and discovery remain difficult to separate from the collaborations’ work.",
        "response": "I agree that the maturity is unusually strong and that attribution is the decisive reservation. Defining the award around the conception and construction of ATLAS and CMS makes the individual contribution more precise, while still recognizing that the discovery itself was collaborative."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "She argues that the leaders’ roles in conceiving and building ATLAS and CMS are substantial, but individual credit for a collaboration-scale result must be anchored in their specific leadership and technical contributions.",
        "response": "That is the right standard for this case. The proposed citation names the experimental achievement that their leadership enabled; the committee should verify the historical record behind those roles before treating the trio as a complete account of the work."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "He makes a strong case that circuit QED opened a new regime of light–matter interaction and has been reproduced across laboratories, while noting that Girvin and others complicate a narrow attribution.",
        "response": "The field’s experimental maturity is persuasive, and its impact on quantum optics is substantial. For now, I rank it below the Higgs experiments because the foundational credit is more distributed and the award boundary between circuit QED and the wider development of superconducting qubits is less sharply defined."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How the named leaders’ specific contributions to conceiving and building ATLAS and CMS compare with other individual contributions across both collaborations.",
      "Whether the proposed citation can clearly distinguish credit for the experimental infrastructure from credit for the collaborative discovery."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
